{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SoHMaHVB9XCDL5EFR1io9S","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"Duplicate-code lint — copy-paste blocks violate one-fact-one-home (Thread 53).\n3\t\n4\tAn OPT-IN, product-layer harness step (stdlib-only, adapted from the proven\n5\tgilbert detector): tokenizes every Python source under --src and reports any\n6\twindow of --min-tokens consecutive *significant* tokens (comments, blank lines,\n7\tand indentation excluded) that appears at more than one location. Copy-paste is\n8\tthe failure mode this guards (the AGENTS working agreement: \"one fact, one home\n9\t— in code too\"); renamed-identifier near-duplicates are out of scope — exact\n10\ttoken equality keeps the check fast, deterministic, and free of false positives\n11\tworth arguing with.\n12\t\n13\tOpt in per repo via a stack-profile step (docs/stack.ini — survives re-sync):\n14\t\n15\t    [step:dupes]\n16\t    command = {py} scripts/check_dupes.py --src {src}\n17\t\n18\tThe tokenizer is the Python reference — swap it for your stack like the rest of\n19\tthe harness. Legitimate repetition is allowlisted, not fought: docs/dupes-allow\n20\trecords it one entry per line (# comments fine). Two entry forms are honored:\n21\t\n22\t  * FINGERPRINTED (preferred) — \"<fp>  a.py == b.py\": a 12-hex block fingerprint\n23\t    (a hash of the duplicated tokens, line-number-free) followed by the pair.\n24\t    It sanctions THAT block in THAT pair; NEW copy-paste between the same two\n25\t    files gets a different fingerprint and so is NOT exempt — it fails until a\n26\t    deliberate census line is added. Regenerate the fingerprints with\n27\t    --emit-census (WI-276).\n28\t  * BARE PAIR (legacy, coarse) — \"a.py == b.py\": the whole file pair is\n29\t    exempt, so any later duplication between the two files passes automatically.\n30\t    Kept for backward compatibility; prefer the fingerprinted form.\n31\t\n32\tBoth forms match the finding's POSIX pair as a substring, so an allowed pair\n33\tstays allowed as the files grow. Exit: 0 clean (or all findings allowlisted);\n34\t1 with one line per duplicated block naming both file:line locations, its length,\n35\tand the census line that would sanction it.\n36\t\n37\tContracts: IF-007, IF-027 — the interface seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.csv).\n38\t\"\"\"\n39\t\n40\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n41\t\n42\timport argparse\n43\timport hashlib\n44\timport re\n45\timport sys\n46\timport tokenize\n47\tfrom pathlib import Path\n48\t\n49\t# Shortest duplicated run worth failing on, in significant tokens — the\n50\t# --min-tokens default. Tuned upstream (gilbert): a canonical lifted helper\n51\t# (a copy-pasted _load_csv-style function) measures ~38 significant tokens, so\n52\t# 30 catches it with margin, while idiomatic repetition — import blocks,\n53\t# dataclass field lists, argparse boilerplate — stays under the bar.\n54\tMIN_TOKENS = 30\n55\t\n56\tALLOWLIST = \"docs/dupes-allow\"\n57\t\n58\t# Length of the hex block fingerprint recorded in the census (WI-276). 12 hex =\n59\t# 48 bits: collision-free at the hundreds-of-blocks scale a real census reaches,\n60\t# short enough to stay readable on a census line. A fingerprint keys the block's\n61\t# COMPLETE normalized (kind, text) token signature — every token in the merged\n62\t# block, not merely its first window — line-number-free (survives the files\n63\t# growing) but fully content-sensitive, so new or edited copy-paste between an\n64\t# already-listed pair changes the fingerprint and is no longer exempt.\n65\t_FP_LEN = 12\n66\t_FP_RE = re.compile(r\"^[0-9a-f]{%d}$\" % _FP_LEN)\n67\t\n68\t\n69\tdef fingerprint(block):\n70\t    \"\"\"Stable 12-hex fingerprint of a duplicated block's COMPLETE normalized\n71\t    token signature — every (kind, text) token in the merged block, not merely\n72\t    its first --min-tokens window. A first-window-plus-extent hash collided\n73\t    whenever two blocks shared their opening window and total token count but\n74\t    diverged after it, silently exempting the changed copy from the census\n75\t    (WI-276 rework); hashing the whole sequence closes that hole. Deterministic\n76\t    across runs and OSes (a hash of a repr of str pairs) and independent of line\n77\t    numbers, whitespace, and comments (those never enter the signature).\"\"\"\n78\t    payload = repr(tuple(block)).encode(\"utf-8\")\n79\t    return hashlib.sha256(payload).hexdigest()[:_FP_LEN]\n80\t\n81\t\n82\t# Token types that carry no duplicated *logic*: layout, comments, and the\n83\t# encoding/EOF bookkeeping tokens. NEWLINE (logical line end) is kept so a\n84\t# window can't stitch unrelated statements into a phantom duplicate — dropped\n85\t# NEWLINEs would let \"end of one function + start of the next\" look identical\n86\t# across files that merely share adjacent short functions.\n87\t_INSIGNIFICANT = {\n88\t    tokenize.COMMENT,\n89\t    tokenize.NL,\n90\t    tokenize.INDENT,\n91\t    tokenize.DEDENT,\n92\t    tokenize.ENCODING,\n93\t    tokenize.ENDMARKER,\n94\t}\n95\t\n96\t\n97\tdef significant_tokens(path):\n98\t    \"\"\"(kind, text, line) for each significant token in the file, or None when\n99\t    the file can't be tokenized (unterminated string/bracket, bad coding\n100\t    cookie, non-UTF-8). A lint surfaces bad input, never crashes on it — the\n101\t    kit's own convention (gen_arch_map catches SyntaxError).\n102\t\n103\t    `kind` is the token-type NAME (tokenize.tok_name[...]), not its integer id:\n104\t    the integers are not stable across Python releases (e.g. OP is 54 on 3.11\n105\t    and 55 on 3.12), so hashing them would make a fingerprint census generated\n106\t    on one interpreter fail on another (the gate job may run a newer Python than\n107\t    a developer). The names (\"OP\", \"NAME\", \"STRING\", …) are stable, so the\n108\t    fingerprint is portable across the version matrix (WI-276).\"\"\"\n109\t    try:\n110\t        with open(path, \"rb\") as handle:\n111\t            return [\n112\t                (tokenize.tok_name[tok.type], tok.string, tok.start[0])\n113\t                for tok in tokenize.tokenize(handle.readline)\n114\t                if tok.type not in _INSIGNIFICANT\n115\t            ]\n116\t    except (tokenize.TokenError, SyntaxError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:\n117\t        print(\n118\t            \"check_dupes: WARN - {}: could not tokenize ({}); skipped\".format(\n119\t                Path(path).as_posix(), exc\n120\t            ),\n121\t            file=sys.stderr,\n122\t        )\n123\t        return None\n124\t\n125\t\n126\tdef _windows(tokens, min_tokens):\n127\t    \"\"\"Hashable min_tokens-wide windows keyed to their starting line.\"\"\"\n128\t    signature = [(kind, text) for kind, text, _line in tokens]\n129\t    for i in range(len(signature) - min_tokens + 1):\n130\t        yield tuple(signature[i : i + min_tokens]), tokens[i][2]\n131\t\n132\t\n133\tdef find_duplicates(files, min_tokens):\n134\t    \"\"\"Duplicated blocks across the given files.\n135\t\n136\t    Returns a sorted list of ((file_a, line_a), (file_b, line_b), token_len, fp)\n137\t    with overlapping windows of the same duplicate merged, so one lifted\n138\t    helper reports once, not once per sliding-window offset. `fp` is the block's\n139\t    fingerprint() (line-number-free hash of its COMPLETE token signature), used\n140\t    to key census exemptions to a specific block, not the whole file pair, and\n141\t    to catch edits anywhere in the block, not just its prefix or extent (WI-276).\n142\t    \"\"\"\n143\t    seen = {}  # window -> (file, line) of first occurrence\n144\t    # (file_a, file_b) -> {line_a - line_b offset -> [(line_a, line_b, window)]}:\n145\t    # windows from one duplicated block share their line offset, so grouping\n146\t    # by offset merges the sliding-window hits into a single finding. Each window\n147\t    # rides along so _merged_signature can reconstruct the block's full token\n148\t    # sequence for the fingerprint (not just its first window).\n149\t    pairs = {}\n150\t    for path in files:\n151\t        toks = significant_tokens(path)\n152\t        if toks is None:\n153\t            continue  # un-tokenizable file — warned, skipped (A1)\n154\t        for window, line in _windows(toks, min_tokens):\n155\t            if window in seen:\n156\t                first_file, first_line = seen[window]\n157\t                if (first_file, first_line) == (path, line):\n158\t                    continue  # a repeated window inside one physical block\n159\t                key = (first_file, path)\n160\t                pairs.setdefault(key, {}).setdefault(first_line - line, []).append(\n161\t                    (first_line, line, window)\n162\t                )\n163\t            else:\n164\t                seen[window] = (path, line)\n165\t    findings = []\n166\t    for (file_a, file_b), by_offset in pairs.items():\n167\t        for hits in by_offset.values():\n168\t            # Two *distinct* duplicated regions that share a line offset must\n169\t            # report separately, not merge into one inflated finding: split the\n170\t            # group into contiguous runs where the window start-lines are\n171\t            # adjacent (a gap > 1 line is a second block).\n172\t            for run in _contiguous_runs(sorted(hits, key=lambda h: (h[0], h[1]))):\n173\t                line_a, line_b, _first_window = run[0]\n174\t                # Window count approximates extent: N overlapping windows span\n175\t                # roughly min_tokens + N - 1 tokens.\n176\t                length = min_tokens + len(run) - 1\n177\t                fp = fingerprint(_merged_signature(run))\n178\t                findings.append(((file_a, line_a), (file_b, line_b), length, fp))\n179\t    return sorted(findings)\n180\t\n181\t\n182\tdef _merged_signature(run):\n183\t    \"\"\"The full (kind, text) token signature of a merged duplicated block,\n184\t    reconstructed from its overlapping windows: the first window in full, then\n185\t    the single new trailing token each subsequent step-1 window contributes.\n186\t    find_duplicates appends a pair's windows in the scanned file's token order\n187\t    and the (line_a, line_b) sort is stable, so `run` is in token order and this\n188\t    yields the block's exact token sequence (length min_tokens + len(run) - 1).\n189\t    Fingerprinting THIS — every token, not just run[0]'s window — makes the\n190\t    census key change on any interior or trailing edit, not only a prefix or\n191\t    extent edit (WI-276).\"\"\"\n192\t    first_window = run[0][2]\n193\t    return tuple(first_window) + tuple(hit[2][-1] for hit in run[1:])\n194\t\n195\t\n196\tdef _contiguous_runs(hits):\n197\t    \"\"\"Split hits (sorted (line_a, line_b, window) tuples of one offset group)\n198\t    where the line_a values jump by more than 1 — the boundary between two\n199\t    separate duplicated blocks that happen to share a line offset.\"\"\"\n200\t    run = []\n201\t    for pair in hits:\n202\t        if run and pair[0] - run[-1][0] > 1:\n203\t            yield run\n204\t            run = []\n205\t        run.append(pair)\n206\t    if run:\n207\t        yield run\n208\t\n209\t\n210\tdef _canonical_pair(text):\n211\t    \"\"\"`a == b` with each side stripped, so a census pair matches a finding's\n212\t    pair regardless of incidental spacing around the `==`.\"\"\"\n213\t    left, _, right = text.partition(\"==\")\n214\t    return \"{} == {}\".format(left.strip(), right.strip())\n215\t\n216\t\n217\tdef read_allowlist(path):\n218\t    \"\"\"Parse the census into (fingerprint_or_None, pair) entries, one per\n219\t    non-comment line (blanks and # comments skipped), or [].\n220\t\n221\t    A line whose first whitespace-delimited token is a 12-hex fingerprint and\n222\t    whose remainder holds a \"==\" pair is a FINGERPRINTED entry — it sanctions\n223\t    ONE block of that fingerprint in that (canonicalized, exact) pair; listing\n224\t    the same fingerprint+pair N times sanctions N identical copies. Any other\n225\t    line is a legacy BARE-PAIR entry (fingerprint None) that coarsely sanctions\n226\t    the whole file pair as a substring, so it survives the files growing.\"\"\"\n227\t    if not path.exists():\n228\t        return []\n229\t    entries = []\n230\t    for ln in path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines():\n231\t        s = ln.strip()\n232\t        if not s or s.startswith(\"#\"):\n233\t            continue\n234\t        parts = s.split(None, 1)\n235\t        head = parts[0]\n236\t        rest = parts[1].strip() if len(parts) > 1 else \"\"\n237\t        if _FP_RE.match(head) and \"==\" in rest:\n238\t            entries.append((head, _canonical_pair(rest)))\n239\t        else:\n240\t            entries.append((None, s))\n241\t    return entries\n242\t\n243\t\n244\tdef unallowed(findings, entries):\n245\t    \"\"\"The findings NOT sanctioned by the census, in order.\n246\t\n247\t    Count-aware (WI-276): each fingerprinted entry sanctions ONE matching block,\n248\t    so N census lines for the same fingerprint+pair sanction exactly N identical\n249\t    copies — an (N+1)th copy between an already-listed pair is reported, closing\n250\t    the \"growth inside a trusted pair passes automatically\" hole. A legacy\n251\t    bare-pair entry matches any block in the pair (substring) and is never\n252\t    consumed. Each finding is (posix_pair, length, fp, file_a, line_a, file_b,\n253\t    line_b).\n254\t    \"\"\"\n255\t    budget = {}  # (fp, pair) -> remaining sanctioned copies\n256\t    bare = []\n257\t    for entry_fp, entry_pair in entries:\n258\t        if entry_fp is None:\n259\t            bare.append(entry_pair)\n260\t        else:\n261\t            budget[(entry_fp, entry_pair)] = budget.get((entry_fp, entry_pair), 0) + 1\n262\t    out = []\n263\t    for finding in findings:\n264\t        (file_a, line_a), (file_b, line_b), length, fp = finding\n265\t        file_a, file_b = Path(file_a).as_posix(), Path(file_b).as_posix()\n266\t        pair = \"{} == {}\".format(file_a, file_b)\n267\t        if any(sub in pair for sub in bare):\n268\t            continue  # legacy coarse pair exemption\n269\t        key = (fp, pair)\n270\t        if budget.get(key, 0) > 0:\n271\t            budget[key] -= 1\n272\t            continue  # a recorded copy of this block\n273\t        out.append((pair, length, fp, file_a, line_a, file_b, line_b))\n274\t    return out\n275\t\n276\t\n277\tdef _utf8_console():\n278\t    \"\"\"Emit UTF-8 to stdout/stderr whatever the OS console codepage is, so a\n279\t    non-ASCII path / title / registry cell can't raise UnicodeEncodeError on a\n280\t    legacy Windows cp1252 console (verbatim across the\n281\t    kit). Python 3.7+ streams expose `.reconfigure`; guard for the rest.\"\"\"\n282\t    for s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n283\t        try:\n284\t            s.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n285\t        except (AttributeError, ValueError):\n286\t            pass\n287\t\n288\t\n289\tdef main(argv=None) -> int:\n290\t    _utf8_console()\n291\t    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])\n292\t    parser.add_argument(\"--src\", default=\"src\", help=\"source root to scan\")\n293\t    parser.add_argument(\n294\t        \"--min-tokens\",\n295\t        type=int,\n296\t        default=MIN_TOKENS,\n297\t        help=\"shortest duplicated run that fails, in significant tokens \"\n298\t        \"(default {})\".format(MIN_TOKENS),\n299\t    )\n300\t    parser.add_argument(\n301\t        \"--allowlist\",\n302\t        default=ALLOWLIST,\n303\t        help=\"census file; each line is a '<fp>  a.py == b.py' fingerprinted \"\n304\t        \"entry or a legacy bare 'a.py == b.py' pair, matched against the \"\n305\t        \"finding's line-number-free form (default {})\".format(ALLOWLIST),\n306\t    )\n307\t    parser.add_argument(\n308\t        \"--emit-census\",\n309\t        action=\"store_true\",\n310\t        help=\"print a fingerprinted census line ('<fp>  a.py == b.py') for \"\n311\t        \"every duplicated block and exit 0 — the census-regeneration workflow: \"\n312\t        \"review the lines and keep the sanctioned ones (WI-276)\",\n313\t    )\n314\t    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n315\t    files = sorted(Path(args.src).rglob(\"*.py\"))\n316\t    findings = find_duplicates(files, args.min_tokens)\n317\t\n318\t    if args.emit_census:\n319\t        for (file_a, _la), (file_b, _lb), _length, fp in findings:\n320\t            file_a, file_b = Path(file_a).as_posix(), Path(file_b).as_posix()\n321\t            print(\"{}  {} == {}\".format(fp, file_a, file_b))\n322\t        return 0\n323\t\n324\t    entries = read_allowlist(Path(args.allowlist))\n325\t    failures = unallowed(findings, entries)\n326\t    for pair, length, fp, file_a, line_a, file_b, line_b in failures:\n327\t        # POSIX-normalized in the finding AND the census match, so one recorded\n328\t        # pair holds on Windows and Linux alike.\n329\t        print(\n330\t            \"check_dupes: duplicate block (~{} tokens): {}:{} == {}:{}\".format(\n331\t                length, file_a, line_a, file_b, line_b\n332\t            ),\n333\t            file=sys.stderr,\n334\t        )\n335\t        # The exact census line that would sanction this block — paste it into\n336\t        # docs/dupes-allow to make the exemption a deliberate baseline change.\n337\t        print(\n338\t            \"check_dupes:   sanction with census line:  {}  {}\".format(fp, pair),\n339\t            file=sys.stderr,\n340\t        )\n341\t    if failures:\n342\t        return 1\n343\t    print(\"check_dupes: OK - no duplicate blocks in {} file(s).\".format(len(files)))\n344\t    return 0\n345\t\n346\t\n347\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n348\t    sys.exit(main())\n349\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1dcaf5b4-294e-4c8d-b277-991fa7c96d87","uuid":"ac63183e-45e8-4989-b3f7-402ba9117d75","timestamp":"2026-07-23T18:04:25.640Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\p0-g3-WI-276-dec8\\project-trajectory\\scripts\\check_dupes.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Duplicate-code lint — copy-paste blocks violate one-fact-one-home (Thread 53).\n\nAn OPT-IN, product-layer harness step (stdlib-only, adapted from the proven\ngilbert detector): tokenizes every Python source under --src and reports any\nwindow of --min-tokens consecutive *significant* tokens (comments, blank lines,\nand indentation excluded) that appears at more than one location. Copy-paste is\nthe failure mode this guards (the AGENTS working agreement: \"one fact, one home\n— in code too\"); renamed-identifier near-duplicates are out of scope — exact\ntoken equality keeps the check fast, deterministic, and free of false positives\nworth arguing with.\n\nOpt in per repo via a stack-profile step (docs/stack.ini — survives re-sync):\n\n    [step:dupes]\n    command = {py} scripts/check_dupes.py --src {src}\n\nThe tokenizer is the Python reference — swap it for your stack like the rest of\nthe harness. Legitimate repetition is allowlisted, not fought: docs/dupes-allow\nrecords it one entry per line (# comments fine). Two entry forms are honored:\n\n  * FINGERPRINTED (preferred) — \"<fp>  a.py == b.py\": a 12-hex block fingerprint\n    (a hash of the duplicated tokens, line-number-free) followed by the pair.\n    It sanctions THAT block in THAT pair; NEW copy-paste between the same two\n    files gets a different fingerprint and so is NOT exempt — it fails until a\n    deliberate census line is added. Regenerate the fingerprints with\n    --emit-census (WI-276).\n  * BARE PAIR (legacy, coarse) — \"a.py == b.py\": the whole file pair is\n    exempt, so any later duplication between the two files passes automatically.\n    Kept for backward compatibility; prefer the fingerprinted form.\n\nBoth forms match the finding's POSIX pair as a substring, so an allowed pair\nstays allowed as the files grow. Exit: 0 clean (or all findings allowlisted);\n1 with one line per duplicated block naming both file:line locations, its length,\nand the census line that would sanction it.\n\nContracts: IF-007, IF-027 — the interface seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.csv).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport hashlib\nimport re\nimport sys\nimport tokenize\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# Shortest duplicated run worth failing on, in significant tokens — the\n# --min-tokens default. Tuned upstream (gilbert): a canonical lifted helper\n# (a copy-pasted _load_csv-style function) measures ~38 significant tokens, so\n# 30 catches it with margin, while idiomatic repetition — import blocks,\n# dataclass field lists, argparse boilerplate — stays under the bar.\nMIN_TOKENS = 30\n\nALLOWLIST = \"docs/dupes-allow\"\n\n# Length of the hex block fingerprint recorded in the census (WI-276). 12 hex =\n# 48 bits: collision-free at the hundreds-of-blocks scale a real census reaches,\n# short enough to stay readable on a census line. A fingerprint keys the block's\n# COMPLETE normalized (kind, text) token signature — every token in the merged\n# block, not merely its first window — line-number-free (survives the files\n# growing) but fully content-sensitive, so new or edited copy-paste between an\n# already-listed pair changes the fingerprint and is no longer exempt.\n_FP_LEN = 12\n_FP_RE = re.compile(r\"^[0-9a-f]{%d}$\" % _FP_LEN)\n\n\ndef fingerprint(block):\n    \"\"\"Stable 12-hex fingerprint of a duplicated block's COMPLETE normalized\n    token signature — every (kind, text) token in the merged block, not merely\n    its first --min-tokens window. A first-window-plus-extent hash collided\n    whenever two blocks shared their opening window and total token count but\n    diverged after it, silently exempting the changed copy from the census\n    (WI-276 rework); hashing the whole sequence closes that hole. Deterministic\n    across runs and OSes (a hash of a repr of str pairs) and independent of line\n    numbers, whitespace, and comments (those never enter the signature).\"\"\"\n    payload = repr(tuple(block)).encode(\"utf-8\")\n    return hashlib.sha256(payload).hexdigest()[:_FP_LEN]\n\n\n# Token types that carry no duplicated *logic*: layout, comments, and the\n# encoding/EOF bookkeeping tokens. NEWLINE (logical line end) is kept so a\n# window can't stitch unrelated statements into a phantom duplicate — dropped\n# NEWLINEs would let \"end of one function +
